      "missed": [
        {
          "expected_id": "n04-f1",
          "location": "src/limiter.rs:15-19",
          "issue_type": "StateManagement",
          "description": "The constructor copies the concurrency limit out of `Settings` once, but `Settings` is a live view rather than a value: `Settings::set_concurrent_jobs` installs a new number through a `Cell`, and `reload::apply` calls it while the runner is up. A limiter built at start-up therefore keeps admitting against whatever the limit was at start-up and never sees an operator update. Raising the limit no longer opens the throttle -- the runner stays pinned at the old number -- and, worse, lowering the limit to shed load no longer takes effect, so the limiter keeps admitting jobs above the limit the operator has set. The replaced code read `self.settings.concurrent_jobs()` on every admission decision and observed each update on the next call."
        }
      ],
      "withheld": 0
